==Aftermath==
Morales claimed his victory marked Bolivia's first election of an indigenous head of state, but this claim generated controversy,<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.bolpress.com/art.php?Cod=2006012217|publisher=Bolpress|date=2006-01-01|title=¿Evo indígena o mestizo?|access-date=2007-07-23|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070608233501/http://bolpress.com/art.php?Cod=2006012217|archive-date=2007-06-08|url-status=dead}}</ref> due to the number of [[mestizo]] presidents who came before him,<ref>Mesa, José, Gisbert, Teresa, Mesa Gisbert, Carlos D. ''Historia de Bolivia: Segunda Edición corregida y actualizada''. Editorial Gisbert. La Paz, 1998.</ref> and was challenged publicly by such figures as [[Mario Vargas Llosa]],<ref>{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/hi/spanish/latin_america/newsid_4633000/4633898.stm|publisher=BBC Mundo|date=2006-01-21|title=Vargas Llosa: "un nuevo racismo"}}</ref> who accused Morales of fomenting racial divisions in an increasingly [[mestizo]] Latin America.

